# Hardware enablement beyond the kernel and the firmware (plan §5.2, §12).
#
# WHAT "THE DRIVERS WORK" ACTUALLY NEEDS
# --------------------------------------
# Three separate things decide whether a machine feels supported, and none of them is a
# driver written by this project:
#
#   1. the kernel and CPU microcode (10-kernel.list.chroot);
#   2. the firmware blobs (20-firmware.list.chroot);
#   3. the userspace stack listed here, without which a machine can have the driver and
#      still not be able to use the hardware: no VA-API driver means every video is
#      decoded by the CPU; no zram means a 4 GB machine starts swapping early.
#
# Everything in this list is from the pinned Debian release, and every name is verified
# against the archive by scripts/verify-package-availability.py before any build.
#
# "Is it there, and does this machine actually have it?" is answered by the boot
# self-test: boot any AstraOS medium with astraos.ci=1 on the kernel command line and the
# machine reports what it found (docs/BUILDING.md, "The boot self-test").

# --- Hardware video decode (VA-API) -------------------------------------------
# Without these, Firefox and mpv decode 1080p in software; on a two-core machine the
# video drops frames at full screen. With them, decode runs on the GPU. All three
# vendors' current drivers are here because the same image boots everywhere.

# Intel, Gen8 (Broadwell) and newer.
intel-media-va-driver
# Intel, Gen7 and older, still in the field on machines exactly like this project's
# 2011 Sandy Bridge development laptop.
i965-va-driver
# AMD (and Intel through the Gallium driver).
mesa-va-drivers
# The DRM2 backend all of the above use.
libva-drm2
# VA-API sink for the GStreamer players (parole).
gstreamer1.0-vaapi
# Prints what this machine actually supports: the first command in every hardware
# decode problem.
vainfo

# --- Firmware updates -----------------------------------------------------------
# Firmware from the Linux Vendor Firmware Service: SSDs, docks, laptops, controllers.
# The user runs "fwupdmgr get-updates" when they want them; nothing is fetched on its
# own, and nothing is flashed without a confirmation.
fwupd

# --- Memory and interrupts on real machines -------------------------------------
# Compressed swap in RAM. On the 4 GB machines this release targets it is the single
# biggest responsiveness win (docs/HARDWARE_COMPATIBILITY.md).
zram-tools
# Spreads device interrupts over cores on the machines where that helps.
irqbalance

# --- Bluetooth -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firmware for the older Broadcom/CSR USB dongles that keep turning up. The stack
# itself (bluez, blueman, libspa-0.2-bluetooth) is in 30-desktop.
bluez-firmware

# --- Phones, cameras and music players over USB -----------------------------------
# MTP is what Android phones speak when plugged in; PTP is what cameras speak.
# gvfs-backends (25-graphics) does the file manager integration, libmtp-runtime the
# device probing, mtp-tools the command-line diagnosis.
libmtp-runtime
mtp-tools
# Android platform tools (adb, fastboot) with the udev rules that let them talk to a
# phone without root. This is the foundation for the phone-as-microphone work and for
# sideloading.
adb
fastboot
android-sdk-platform-tools-common

# --- Filesystems people plug in ---------------------------------------------------
# Windows disks, read-write.
ntfs-3g
# The exFAT default on SD cards and cameras.
exfatprogs
# Read the images this project builds, and other distributions'.
squashfs-tools

# --- Printing hardware -------------------------------------------------------------
# cups, cups-filters, the PDF queue and system-config-printer are in 30-desktop; these
# two driver families cover the largest share of consumer printers.
printer-driver-gutenprint
printer-driver-escpr
